D.2. Egoroff’s theorem
Egoroff’s theorem is an important result in measure theory and establishes a condition for measurable functions to be uniformly continuous in an almost full-measure set.
Theorem 6 (Egoroff’s theorem [Bog07] theorem 2.2.1). Let (X, A, μ) be a space with a finite nonnegative measure μ and let μ-measurable functions fn be such that μalmost everywhere there is a finite limit f(x) := limn→∞ fn(x). Then, for every ε > 0, there exists a set Xε ∈ A such that μ (X \ Xε) < ε and the functions fn converge to f uniformly on Xε.
